@flexiarg creativity-commons/recognised-right-to-self-organise
@title Recognised Right To Self-Organise
@keywords autonomy, external authority, programmer, creative acts, Ostrom 7
@audience operators, institution designers, agent builders
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 7 (minimal recognition of rights to organise), as transposed in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016), Table 2 and section "7. Minimal recognition of rights to organise" (Colton 2012; Colton et al. 2014). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@transposes institutions/minimal-recognition-of-rights-to-organise
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/affected-participants-change-the-rules creativity-commons/nested-places]

! conclusion:
  The rights of Producers to devise institutions governing their
  contributions are not challenged by external authorities, and how far
  this holds is measured, not asserted.

  + context:
    In computational settings the foremost external authority is the
    programmer (or operator). Teaching systems how to devise institutions
    is at least as relevant as teaching them to conform to given
    instructions.

  + IF:
    Agents or contributors work under rules set by someone outside the
    group doing the work.

  + HOWEVER:
    Early on, outside direction is needed and right; a claim of autonomy
    that the record does not support is the "mini me" critique waiting
    to happen.

  + THEN:
    Count changes to the working rules by origin: operator- or
    programmer-made versus made by the system itself. Identify the
    individual acts (a rule proposed, a test changed, a pattern revised)
    and attribute each one. Report the ratio over time.

  + BECAUSE:
    The frequency of system-originated changes compared with
    operator-originated ones is a test of how far the right to organise
    is actually exercised.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: a GPL fork is an exercise of this right relative to upstream; the FUTON Emacs fork organises its own fix rules without challenging GNU's.]
    next[Trial: classify a week of CLAUDE.md and pattern edits by origin (Joe vs agent) from commit trailers.]
